/*
RMSTEST_38 - does clumping_factor control land CONNECTEDNESS
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 16 (Sec.6.1's land-growth model)

THE QUESTION
RMSTEST_28a/28b showed a single create_land coming back in several disconnected
pieces - at cf -5 (3/5/4 pieces) and, decisively, at cf 0 in the control
(4/4/1). A region grown by frontier expansion from one origin CANNOT fragment,
so "one origin, one frontier" is wrong. Re-checked under 8-connectivity, so it
is not the probe's connectivity choice: the pieces are genuinely disconnected
even allowing diagonal adjacency.

What that leaves unanswered is whether fragmentation is a PROPERTY OF LOW
CLUMPING or a property of land growth in general. Both runs so far sat at the
low end, which is exactly why the effect looked absolute rather than graded.

WORKING HYPOTHESIS, from community observation plus our own measurement
Community testing (AoK Heaven, "The Cartographer: RMS Discoveries") puts the
accepted range at roughly -100 to 99 rather than the conventional 1-15, with
negatives giving "super spindly" lands and values near +100 giving "very clumped
and roundish" ones. Our tracked corpus uses -20 through 100. Put beside the
fragmentation, that suggests:

  clumping_factor controls how strongly growth prefers tiles ADJACENT to the
  existing mass, and at low or negative values the land may place tiles
  DETACHED from it.

That is "several seeds scattered and grown separately" expressed as one
parameter rather than as a separate seeding stage. If it holds, Sec.6.1's weight
buckets survive intact and the only change is that the neighborsOwned = 0 bucket
stops being empty.

METHOD - ONE MAP, SIX LANDS, NO INTERACTION
Six lands, one per clumping_factor value, each with its own terrain so the probe
can separate them, placed far enough apart that they never meet. Every other
attribute is identical and matches RMSTEST_28a/28b exactly - number_of_tiles 400,
base_size 1, border_fuzziness 0 - so the new runs are directly comparable to the
old ones rather than being a fresh baseline.

  SNOW    cf -20    beyond anything measured
  DESERT  cf   0    reproduces the RMSTEST_28b control
  DIRT    cf   8    the documented default
  DIRT2   cf  20    the corpus mode
  DIRT3   cf  40    high
  GRASS3  cf 100    corpus uses this 18 times, above the community's stated 99

Generated at Giant (252) = 63504 tiles, so six 400-tile lands occupy under 4% of
the map. Positions are on a 3 x 2 grid 76 and 101 tiles apart; a compact 400-tile
land has a radius near 11 tiles and even a badly spindly one should not span
half that separation. **Check this rather than assume it** - the read-off below
includes a bounding-box overlap check, and if any two lands touch, that pair is
void and needs re-running singly.

Why one map instead of six single-land scripts: six scripts at three runs each is
eighteen generations, this is three. The cost is the no-interaction assumption,
which is checkable from the export itself, so it is a cheap trade.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW
  ...and once per terrain: DESERT, DIRT, DIRT2, DIRT3, GRASS3.
  For each: piece COUNT, the size distribution, and circularity.
  Also confirm total tiles per terrain sits near 400 (+ the ~3% growth overshoot
  of item 7d); a total far below 400 would mean growth terminated early, which
  is a different finding and would invalidate the piece count.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. THE ONE THAT MATTERS: piece count falls MONOTONICALLY as cf rises, reaching
   1 somewhere in the corpus's common band of 15-25. That confirms the
   hypothesis, and Sec.6.1 needs a changed weight table rather than a new
   pipeline stage - much the smaller repair.
2. Pieces persist at cf 100. Then growth is not frontier-based at ANY setting and
   Sec.6.1 must be rebuilt from scratch, which is the expensive outcome and the
   reason this test is worth a sitting.
3. Piece count is flat across the whole sweep. Then fragmentation is unrelated to
   clumping and something else entirely is editing land terrain - go looking for
   that before modelling anything.
4. Circularity should rise with cf across the sweep (RMSTEST_20/21 measured it
   ramping smoothly to ~15 then saturating). If circularity saturates while piece
   count keeps falling, connectedness and shape are separate knobs and the single
   clumping_factor parameter drives two mechanisms - worth knowing before fitting
   either.
5. cf 100 is above the community's reported maximum of 99. If its land looks
   identical to cf 40's rather than more clumped, the value is being clamped, and
   that is a free finding about the 18 corpus uses of it.

GENERATE AT GIANT (252) WITH ANY PLAYER COUNT - there are no player lands, so the
count does not enter. RUN IT THREE TIMES, and REGENERATE BETWEEN SAVES: a
previous batch exported the same generated map three times, and the check is that
the per-terrain tile totals differ run to run.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type SNOW
    number_of_tiles 400
    base_size 1
    clumping_factor -20
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 20 20
  }

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DESERT
    number_of_tiles 400
    base_size 1
    clumping_factor 0
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 50 20
  }

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT
    number_of_tiles 400
    base_size 1
    clumping_factor 8
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 80 20
  }

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT2
    number_of_tiles 400
    base_size 1
    clumping_factor 20
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 20 60
  }

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type DIRT3
    number_of_tiles 400
    base_size 1
    clumping_factor 40
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 50 60
  }

  create_land
  {
    terrain_type GRASS3
    number_of_tiles 400
    base_size 1
    clumping_factor 100
    border_fuzziness 0
    land_position 80 60
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

/* NO <CLIFF_GENERATION> HEADER - an empty one generates cliffs, which would put
   terrain 16 under them (guide:1325) and add a second thing moving terrain. */

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
